nto two different use cases  either they feel very familiar with my  sequel and relational databases or they  want to develop no sequel solutions and  so I'm going to talk about both use  cases so that we can talk about those as  architecture well start with no sequel  so the simplest thing you can do is spin  up an app engine application and store  your application data in  data store and your static data in cloud  storage and so this should allow you  with almost no configuration to get up  and running storing data that you can  share it back to a web application or a  mobile application just as a prototype  now if you wanted to go down the  relational database path the way that  you do this is you would spin up compute  engine to use whatever framework you  want now this could be node and we just  introduced node and App Engine this  could be Python Django if you're a  Python developer it could be WordPress  that you're running on a compute engine  instance and then you would again set up  a cloud sequel so you actually have to  think a little bit you have to set up an  instance pick a size and tell us how  much storage you want and then you would  use cloud storage just like you would  with the other solution to store your  static content so this can literally go  from zero to serving applications in an  hour  right this is the simplest way that you  can get up and running on Google cloud  and start serving data now if you take  that concept and you're like oh this is  interesting and I want to scale it out  and start to move it to production and  share it with more than just me and a  couple friends you start to start  getting more complicated architectures  if you're going down to my sequel path  good news is App Engine and datastore  will just scale for you you don't have  to make any changes you just send it  more traffic store more data and it'll  scale for you same thing for cloud  storage these are fully managed leave it  and forget it type applications that  allow you to scale up so your  architecture is very similar for the no  sequel path if you're going down the  relational database path and you need to  scale and let's just assume you have  more traffic that you can put through a  single VM you would put a Google cloud  load balancer there's a couple talks  that would've happened today about this  in front of several VMs running  WordPress Jango node rails whatever  framework you Happiny running and then  speaking back to cloud sequel now with  cloud sequel when you set it up if you  are running in a production environment  you add a failover instance and that  failover instance gives you high  availability so again in a fully managed  way we'll give you a multi instance my  sequel cluster a set of replicas that  handle failover and if for some reason  there's an issue with one of you  or with your primary cloud sequel  instance we will fail over to the  failover replica so that you'll have no  downtime this is all done with a couple  of clicks when you set up your my sequel  instance again cloud storage for all  your static content really the bedrock  of all of these architectures for your  static content great so now I've gotten  up to a gigabyte of data I've got a  bunch of data in my system and what am I  going to do now so if you are continuing  to go down the our DBMS path if you've  got my sequel running you now have lots  of websites and lots of apps all talking  to your application so one thing again  cloud load balancer and you could do  this with H a proxy or and or whatever  your favorite load balancer is but if  you're in the manage world where you  want Google to manager for you and take  advantage of our amazing site  reliability engineering organization  then you would use the cloud load  balancer to put in front of your VMs  again to shard your request traffic to  the various compute pieces and then go  down to the cloud sequel level now cloud  sequel it starts to get more interesting  once you