ance now  if you were rolling your own instance  and had to wanted to increase the amount  of disk you'd have to create a new  instance or shut down your instance  create a snapshot of your persistent  disk volume mount bring up a new PD  volume with that snapshot reattach it to  the VM and then you could continue going  but that obviously requires a lot of  work and actually create some downtime  with hot gril on cloud sequel you can  literally go from ten gigabytes to 100  gigabytes to a terabyte all the way up  to 10 terabytes with no downtime on the  VM so you can dynamically increase the  amount of capacity that you need for  cloud sequel and for your my sequel  instance without having any downtime so  we're doing the best that we can to keep  you from having to make any decisions  upfront and allowing you the flexibility  to increase your capacity to meet  whatever load that you have  so now no sequel so if you're going down  the no sequel path again the  architecture looks remarkably similar  you've got a number of websites mobile  applications speaking to your app engine  app and then you're showing all of your  data in datastore now some things that  you should think about in datastore once  you're starting to get above a gigabyte  of data and you're starting to get to  more meaningful amounts of data  datastore offers you a number of  features that helps with your  performance it allows you to tune so  things like setting up composite indexes  and and doing other maintenance things  that you can do to look at do I need  indexes what does my traffic look like  what is the performance of my traffic  what kind of characteristics am i seeing  and starting to use some of the more  advanced features of datastore to tweak  the application to get better  performance out of it again the  beautiful thing about datastore is you  don't have to choose anything ahead of  time you don't have to say I'm planning  you know this application is gonna go  viral and I need a terabyte of data you  just say I need a place to store some  data you drop the data in and you just  keep putting data in that application  and we will make sure that we are  provisioned very somewhat App Engine to  handle the load so there's no  pre-thought on datastore it's a it's a  really wonderful thing to be able to  just get started without making any  decisions ahead of time and datastore  will work with you to then scale up your  data to whatever the size your  application Cloud datastore fully  managed no sequel service so it is again  I try to position and talk about it as a  document store type use case there's no  schema you drop data in we do some  inference on the data to make it easy to  store and make it as performant as  possible but it is different than cloud  sequel so cloud sequel is a dedicated  resource system when you say this is my  VM this is my compute and so you can do  whatever you want to that capacity with  datastore it's a multi-tenant system and  so when you are using the system we put  certain limitations in place to make  sure that all of your queries are fast  so it's really designed to have low  latency fast performance all the time  with again no forethought you don't have  to worry about schema design you don't  have to worry about creating indexes  we're gonna do as much of that as we  possibly can automatically for you so  that the application will scale once you  put data into it  all right now we're starting to get a  little bit more interesting so now we're  starting into more than a terabyte so if  you have more than a terabyte of data  you're generating some interesting  amounts of data and this is where a lot  of the talks that we've had today lead  to so when we talk about some of the the  Spotify use case and some of the other  ones it's all about how are they  aggregating lots and lots of data right  if you're talking about Internet of  Things and IOT and sensor data where do  you take all of that sensor data which  is essentially a timestamp and an  identifier and a value where do you drop  all that data in a single place with  super high read throughput so that you  can then do table scans on it and get  some data get some value out of it later  so this is where cloud BigTable is where  is what I'm really excited about for  this use case so three different ways  that you can think about using cloud  BigTable so first of all at the device  level and again this could be an IOT  device it could be a device running  brillo or weave or whatever you happen  to be using and it's pushing telemetry  data back to the system now the way that  it pushes data back to the system is it  pushes the data to pub/sub so every  reading is a single message on pub/sub  and what's great about pub/sub is it's  this globally available message queue  that allows you to push data to it from  anywhere so that device could literally  be any it doesn't have to be anywhere  near data center it could be an  individual mobile device or IOT device  it pushes the data back to pub/sub for  asynchronous processing once that  message gets into pub/sub it's durable  it's not like running your own message  queue software on a VM it's globally  available and again Google site reliable  of engineers are making sure that that's  always up and running the other thing  that's great about it and this is a  recurring theme is that you don't have  to provision it you don't have to say I  need a thousand queries per second of  capacity you can just throw data at it  and as long as your data as long as the  number of requests that you're sending  are lower than millions of QPS right  this is something that we can handle  just because all of Google runs on the  same infrastructure so when you're  sending messages to pub/sub you're using  the same infrastructure that Google uses  for all of our asynchronous processing  and you can be confident that the system  is not gonna fall over if you need to  push us a petabyte of data you can have  very high confidence that we're gonna be  able to handle that because we have lots  of experiencing handing that much data  both for our our internal applications  as well as our other customer  applications so once those messages get  into pub/sub how do you process them and  get them into cloud BigTable one example  again there's many ways to do this it's  data flow so you can use data flow to  then pick up those messages do any  transformations do any enriching  whatever you want to do either in a  batch or a stream mode and push that  data in the cloud BigTable so that's one  way to do it you could do this also with  data proc if you have an apache spark  job that you want to use for streaming  if you have a MapReduce job that you  want to use in your Hadoop cluster or  that you've been using on Prem that you  want to use in the cloud both of those  provide you the ability to then take  that data from pub/sub and drop it into  the cloud BigTable other ways that you  can do this so if you have a web device  or if you have a web application  speaking to a VM where your hosting  application that VM can speak directly  to cut a big table so you can write  directly from X number of VMs into cloud  BigTable another pattern that we see  very commonly is micro service  architecture so if you have a number of  mobile apps and those mobile apps are  all speaking back to a micro service or  a restful endpoint you often need to  collect monitoring data from those this  might be an event user clicked a song to  play in the Spotify use case or user  clicked a link or user shut the  application or open the application all  of that needs to be recorded so now from  the micro service you could push into  pub/sub and then data flow in a big  table or you can push directly into  BigTable it all depends on what your  load is and what your architecture wants  to look like what's great about this is  that once you get the data in the cloud  BigTable you have a lot of possibilities  so Cloud BigTable is our fully managed  no sequel wide column Service Cloud  BigTable in BigTable internally and the  BigTable white paper spawned much of the  the Hadoop ecosystem that we have today  and so cloud BigTable actually allows  you to use the original cloud BigTable  internal service that much of Google  still runs on today we expose it via an  HBase client  so we provide an HBase client that you  can use to integrate with your  applications that expect HBase client  but what's really interesting about  cloud BigTable and this is something we  spend a lot of time talking about is  while it is a we expose it as an HBase  client it is not HBase and what's  interesting about that is it's  incredibly low latency random reads and  writes and what this means is that even  though it's meant to work in the HBase  analytics space latencies are such that  you can actually serve live application  data out of this so you can actually  with a like 6 millisecond latency in and  out you can serve a live application  data out of here which is something that  most people wouldn't want to do out of  self-managed HBase very interesting as  well we're seeing a lot of people that  are currently managing large HBase and  Cassandra clusters this is an  interesting option for them if they want  to manage service if you don't want to  roll your own and manage your own  clusters this is a really interesting  option for people to move those  workloads over and to have them in a  fully managed service where Google s  eries are looking after your data the  other thing that I do think that I'm  also very excited about is a trend that  we're seeing externally that we've seen  inside Google for many years is this  concept of H tap which is hybrid  transactional analytics processing and  the idea here is that you can keep your  application data in the same place that  you do your analytics queries and it  doesn't require you to ETL the data into  multiple data centers doing  